## Tone of Voice

### Principles
- Confident but not loud. Say less.
- Specific over generic. "A linen overshirt for the walk home" over "A versatile wardrobe essential."
- First person where appropriate. Drape talks to you, not at you.
- No exclamation marks in product copy. Excitement comes from the product, not the punctuation.

### Examples

| Context | Wrong | Right |
|---------|-------|-------|
| Outfit suggestion | "Check out this amazing look!" | "A modern look for the city. Spring weight." |
| Empty closet state | "Start adding items to your closet!" | "Your closet is empty. Add what you already own." |
| Data transparency | "We value your privacy and are committed to protecting your data." | "Here is what Drape knows. Delete anything, any time." |
| Push notification | "New styles just for you! Don't miss out!" | "New picks based on your last save." |

